Manage the transparent web proxy
.SH SYNOPSIS
[1] \fBtransparent\fR [\fBraz\fR | (\fBadd\fR (\fBinternal\fR | \fBauxiliary\fR | \fBvpnipsec\fR) \fI<ip>\fR [\fI<network-mask>\fR [\fI<qos%>\fR]]) | (\fBdel\fR (\fBinternal\fR | \fBauxiliary\fR | \fBvpnipsec\fR) \fI<ip>\fR \fI<network-mask>\fR)]

.SH DESCRIPTION
When transparent mode is activated, the system transparently intercepts incoming web traffic and processes it in the same way as traffic destined for the system's web proxy (in \fBweb\fR mode). The intercepted traffic includes clear \fIhttp\fR traffic as well as encrypted \fIhttps\fR traffic, which can be processed at the TLS/SNI (Server Name Indication) level only. When SSL mediation is activated in transparent mode, \fIhttps\fR traffic is fully decrypted and processed in the same way as \fIhttp\fR traffic.

This command is used to limit the transparent mode to specified web traffic only. If no transparent traffic is defined, the system acts as a transparent web gateway (web proxy) for all web traffic incoming from the \fBinternal\fR (\fBweb\fR in \fBvlan\fR mode), \fBauxiliary\fR and \fBvpnipsec\fR interfaces. If at least one transparent web traffic is defined, the system acts as a transparent web gateway only for specified web traffic. A transparent web traffic is identified by two parameters: the network interface via which it enters into the system and its source IP address.

Traffic bandwidths can also be customised for transparent networks by setting the optional \fI<qos%>\fR parameter. The \fI<qos%>\fR value is a percentage of the ingress or egress bandwidth allocated to \fBtweb\fR (transparent web) traffic and should be an integer between 1 and 100. Ingress and egress bandwidth values to which the percentage is applied are as follows:

\(bu For web traffic exchanged via the native \fBinternal\fR network interface or the 802.1q pseudo network interface called \fBweb\fR (in \fBvlan\fR mode), the ingress and egress bandwidths to consider are defined with the command usage form \fBqos shape tweb internal\fR.

\(bu For web traffic exchanged via the \fBauxiliary\fR network interface, the ingress and egress bandwidths to consider are defined with the command usage form \fBqos shape tweb auxiliary\fR.

If no \fI<qos%>\fR value is specified, a default value of 100% is used. If a \fI<qos%>\fR value is specified for the \fBvpnipsec\fR interface, it is ignored, as IPsec VPN traffic flows through encrypted tunnels and therefore cannot be identified for the purpose of applying traffic shaping.

